Kamalie broader roadmap and acceptance (@kamalie-roadmap: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @ws-kamalie-games) {
Retained broader roadmap. Tasks here are later milestones, not claims about the initial slice. Standalone-castle.lingua refines KAM.T00 and the first part of KAM.T01; no HUD, menus, permanent powerups or scripted active skills belong to that slice.

## Ordered work and honest dependencies

The owner has selected isolated Castle delivery first. Start with the following contained-game tasks. The later tasks retain the broader exploration and its integration evidence. Promote useful verified shared exports into their owning feature packages when those integrations are undertaken.

### KAM.T00 — First focused Castle with a direct Bevy scene

- [ ] Register Kamalie through the normal native add/store and Castle lifecycle path. Put a small direct Bevy scene with a controllable actor and game camera inside the placed element. Prove GPU presentation, focus entry, input mapping, visible release, resize and removal in the actual canvas. Select and document the execution boundary that isolates game input, time, queries, contacts and teardown from surrounding workspace activity. Inspect the gameplay library dependency boundary. This is the first implementation slice and consumes the existing host APIs. Record concrete host fixes it requires.

### KAM.T01 — Discover the game inside the isolated Castle

- [ ] Evolve T00 into one playable bounded encounter using direct Bevy movement, one enemy family, targeting, damage, death/restart and several materially different builds. Use game-native UI and editable game data for rapid experiments. Start with a small local map and add seeded room/encounter generation when useful to the game loop. Include a versioned run checkpoint. Record the current 2D/3D and real-time/turn choices and playtest observations, keeping room to revise game identity and mechanics. An optional standalone runner can reuse the library for development and performance comparison. This work proceeds on the real Castle host independently of later Workspace feature delivery.

### KAM.T02 — Complete ordinary element behavior and lifecycle

- [ ] Complete ordinary add/select/move/resize/layer/pin behavior for the host's supported controls, keyboard-accessible focus/release, pointer mapping/capture, pause/resume/restart/stop, workspace switch and hidden-state behavior. Persist Castle placement/settings and provide explicit supported run save/resume. Release GPU targets, input, audio and jobs on stop/removal as appropriate to their lifetimes. Run two instances to expose global-state leakage. After T00's boundary is verified, this can overlap T01's gameplay work in separate leaf modules, with shared input/render/runtime edits owned sequentially.

### KAM.T07 — Isolated Castle acceptance and performance

- [ ] Join T00–T02 and complete the first-delivery evidence identified above. Measure KAM.P00 on the actual Castle, fix observed latency/resource problems and exercise ordinary workspace regressions. Publish the source revision, chosen runtime boundary, supported controls, game discovery observations, run-save behavior, measured budgets and remaining limits. This establishes the first usable isolated game milestone. Continue playtesting and optimizing this form while later integrations are considered.

### Subsequent work after the isolated Castle milestone

T03–T06 consume the usable game and the real exports they name. Native build authoring and area integration may proceed alongside each other after their relevant providers are ready. Changes to common gameplay interfaces, rendering, input and physics ownership need one coordinated owner.

### KAM.T03 — Area participation and read-only scenery

- [ ] After isolated Castle acceptance, bind one session to a local area with stable occurrence mappings, role configuration, membership changes and bounded proxy updates. Reuse verified local frames, scoped queries and existing collision/surface exports. Prove the same player and ability systems against ordinary native panels and a live Firefox surface. This can overlap later build-authoring work after both branches have real providers. Shared runtime/input/renderer changes have one owner.

### KAM.T04 — Live movable components and HUD

- [ ] Extend T03 through actual body-owner transfer or shared solver participation, workspace/app mounts and normal focus/lifecycle paths. Push one live Firefox panel, step or jump on the selected support shape, pin an existing call into HUD presentation and operate one Castle as game UI. Exercise editing, resizing, mount changes and competing sessions. This depends on the real mounted-surface and movement-authority exports. Use the existing Avian path for bounded arcade interaction where it is sufficient. Richer independent physics domains consume the Rigid package when those features are needed.

### KAM.T05 — Native build composition

- [ ] Expose T01's implemented game operators through Object Recipes and Behavior Authoring, with Procedural Appearance supplying the chosen effects. Reuse Object Controls for authored actions where supported. Verify definitions against the direct Bevy implementation in the isolated Castle and any supported runner. This branch consumes those authoring exports and can proceed alongside area integration after the isolated Castle milestone and the game definition/operator boundary are proven. Content balance and direct game-native build editing already proceed during T01.

### KAM.T06 — Dungeon and area parity

- [ ] Run the same encounter rules and selected builds in a generated dungeon and a configured workspace arena. Identify environment-specific content and intentional rule differences. Switch cameras on the same session where supported, then test an explicit environment transition separately. Record which state can migrate and the restart path for incompatible state. Add a turn-based scheduler only if that game profile is selected.

### KAM.T08 — Integrated acceptance and comparative evidence

- [ ] Join the later adapters through KAM.W02, the integrated portions of KAM.W03/KAM.W04, KAM.P01 and the relevant KAM.P02 regressions. Repeat isolated Castle acceptance for shared code changes. Publish the consumed source revisions, actual integration support, measured comparisons, unresolved limits and next decision. Advanced character editing, globe/splat integration, CAD, circuits, scientific modeling and camera vision remain optional game extensions with their own provider joins.

## Acceptance experiments

### KAM.W00 — Normal canvas element and focused game

Add Kamalie through the ordinary native add/store flow. Select, move, resize, layer and pin its outer element using supported host controls. Focus and play the inner Bevy scene, release to pan/edit the canvas, type in another application, return to the game and switch workspaces during held input. Exercise pointer capture recovery and keyboard-accessible release. Verify correct local pointer coordinates across resize and device scale, readable game controls, preserved game-local geometry when the outer rectangle moves, and complete cleanup on removal. Reopen saved placement/settings and explicitly resume a supported checkpoint.

### KAM.W01 — Independent native Castle instances

Run two contained Castles with independent random streams, clocks, cameras, controls and run state. Stop or pause one while the other continues through its configured policy. Verify collision and state isolation, repeat a controlled encounter at the supported reproducibility level, and save/load a run with explicit handling of incompatible definitions. If the optional standalone runner exists, use the same definition revision and input sequence there and compare supported rule outcomes.

### KAM.W02 — Workspace participates

Use a bounded room/arena containing Firefox, a live call, a native status/control Castle, one movable prop and an attraction Area. Show obstacle/support interaction, then supported live movement, then HUD/app input. Include one unselected object outside the game region and one overlapping region. Verify membership, ownership and the declared boundary policy throughout.

### KAM.W03 — Concurrent change and recovery

For the first contained delivery, switch focus during held movement, type into Firefox, release pointer capture, hide the game, resize or remove the Castle, edit its outer placement and stop/reset the run while other workspace and app activity continues. Verify input release, correct activity scope, save behavior and stale-generation rejection.

For subsequent area integration, additionally close or resize an enrolled app surface, remove a participant, move the region, transfer movement authority and revoke the session. Verify safe release and selected placement adoption against current revisions through normal undo.

### KAM.W04 — Builds and fidelity

For the first contained delivery, exercise distinct builds configured through direct game data/native Bevy UI, status stacking, cancellation, bounded feedback and a definition revision during play. Lower visual detail while preserving the selected gameplay rules. Confirm that offscreen game entities retain their declared causal activity and that stopping the run releases work.

For subsequent shared authoring, repeat the applicable cases using native recipes and supported controls. Exercise an implemented cheaper behavior model when that integration is delivered, recording changed behavior and transition limits for both contained and enrolled workspace participants.

### KAM.P00 — First Castle performance evidence

Choose target hardware, render resolution, encounter size and frame/input latency budgets before measurement. Instrument gameplay CPU, host/input work, rendering/composition, allocations, resident CPU/GPU bytes and p50/p95/p99 frame time. Exercise resize, active play, two instances, pause, hidden state and removal. Measure resource release and idle behavior. Keep simulation work in direct Bevy paths and investigate expensive host crossings using traces. Use the tiny T00 scene and the real T01 encounter to locate fixed host costs and scaling costs. A minimal runner can later supply an additional baseline. Publish measured results and outstanding optimizations for the isolated Castle milestone.

### KAM.P01 — Comparable cost evidence

During later integration, select target hardware, cadence and latency budgets, scene seed, enemy/projectile counts, render settings, viewport pixels and run duration. Compare the native contained host, integrated static scenery and integrated live surfaces, adding the standalone runner when available. Use a replayable synthetic surface alongside real applications to distinguish the game's costs from varying app load. Measure gameplay CPU, bridge CPU, physics, GPU/render composition, source-update volume, allocations, resident memory, input latency, p50/p95/p99 frame time and idle release. Record work avoided by batching and change-driven updates. Numerical speed claims remain pending until this experiment runs.

### KAM.P02 — Regression and native usability

Exercise existing workspace placement, Area immunity, app focus, mounts, layout and saved-state behavior with the game active and stopped. Keep edit controls reachable under the existing edit-panel-geometry requirement. If Protein is used for game UI, its readable-text and mandatory Laboratory interaction requirements apply. This document's validation checks syntax, links and stated boundaries. Runtime performance and user-facing correctness require the actual native workflows above.
}
